  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Shifts in on‑the‑ground combat power change which armed actor can seize, defend or deny access to onshore and offshore fields, so a rise in an adversary's military strength reduces the ability of external investors or states to hold operational control over those reserves.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">energy_geopolitics_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_force_strength" target="us_iran_gulf_conflict::tanker_incidents" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">An increase in combat-capable forces and battlefield attrition in the Russia‑Ukraine war accelerates arms transfers, training exchanges, and doctrine diffusion to regional actors and proxies, which raises the operational capability and intent to conduct asymmetric attacks on commercial shipping in the Gulf, increasing tanker incidents.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_iran_gulf_conflict</data>
</edge>
