<edge source="monetary_bond_stress::sovereign_borrowing_cost" target="ai_tech_competition::model_capability" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising sovereign borrowing costs force governments to reallocate or cut discretionary budgets and near-term R&amp;D/subsidy programs for strategic technologies, reducing public support for AI research, compute grants and national model-development initiatives.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">monetary_bond_stress</data>
  <data key="d40">ai_tech_competition</data>
</edge>
<edge source="monetary_bond_stress::sovereign_borrowing_cost" target="energy_geopolitics_shift::capital_commitments" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher sovereign borrowing costs raise public debt service and reduce governments' fiscal headroom for co‑financing or guaranteeing oil projects, leading to slower or smaller investor capital commitments into those assets.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">monetary_bond_stress</data>
  <data key="d40">energy_geopolitics_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="monetary_bond_stress::sovereign_borrowing_cost" target="russia_ukraine_war::defense_procurement_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">A rise in sovereign borrowing costs immediately tightens government budgetary space and raises the fiscal price of long-term commitments, prompting governments supporting the Ukraine war to defer, downscale or cancel new defense contracts and thus reduce the procurement rate.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">monetary_bond_stress</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
